# Patch: Work around SIGSEGV in checkpoint saving on GH200/ARM64
#
# Problem:
#   On GH200/ARM64 nodes, distributed checkpoint saves crash intermittently
#   with SIGSEGV inside torch's zip writer (mz_zip_writer_add_mem_ex_v2 ->
#   PyTorchStreamWriter::writeRecord -> torch.serialization._save). See
#   NVIDIA/Megatron-LM#1861. Symptom is the training job dying without a
#   Python traceback the moment it tries to write the first checkpoint.
#
#   Root cause is the multi-process write path in
#   FileSystemWriterAsync.write_preloaded_data_multiproc: it forks one child
#   process per write bucket via mp.get_context("fork"). Forking a process
#   that already holds large CUDA allocations is fragile (copy-on-write
#   pages backed by device memory, plus interactions with the page-locked
#   host buffers torch uses for staging) and corrupts memory inside the
#   child during the zip write. The crash is the symptom; the fork is the
#   cause. The bug is reachable on both --async-save and the synchronous
#   path, because both paths go through write_preloaded_data_multiproc.
#
# What this patch does:
#   Replaces the body of write_preloaded_data_multiproc with a serial
#   in-process loop that calls write_preloaded_data directly for each
#   bucket. The per-bucket logic, the existing exception handling, and the
#   shape of the result placed on global_results_queue are all preserved.
#   No fork, no inter-process queues for the per-bucket workers; plain
#   queue.Queue is used as a stub so we can keep calling write_preloaded_data
#   unchanged. The function still puts a Dict[int, List[WriteResult]] (or
#   an Exception) onto global_results_queue, so the surrounding async-save
#   plumbing is unaffected.
#
#   The trade-off is that each rank now writes its buckets serially.
#   thread_count defaults to 2, so per-rank checkpoint write time is at
#   most ~2x the previous parallel path. Total wall-clock time is dominated
#   by the slowest rank and the device-to-host copy (which already runs
#   before this function), so the user-visible slowdown is small.
#
# Where in the code:
#   File: megatron/core/dist_checkpointing/strategies/filesystem_async.py
#   Function: FileSystemWriterAsync.write_preloaded_data_multiproc()
#   Only the function body is touched; signature and contract are unchanged.
#   `inspect` and `queue` are already imported at the module level.
#
# How to fix if this patch breaks on a future Megatron version:
#   Find write_preloaded_data_multiproc in filesystem_async.py. If it still
#   uses mp.get_context("fork") + ctx.Process to fan out per-bucket writes,
#   replace that fan-out with a serial loop that calls write_preloaded_data
#   in-process for each bucket and aggregates results into the
#   global_results_queue Dict. Keep the contract: dict on success, Exception
#   on failure. If upstream switches to "spawn" or stops forking entirely,
#   drop this patch.
#
# Applied to: Megatron-LM core_v0.16.1
#
diff --git a/megatron/core/dist_checkpointing/strategies/filesystem_async.py b/megatron/core/dist_checkpointing/strategies/filesystem_async.py
